WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.799
Rosa recovers enough to work half days but

00:00:02.799 --> 00:00:05.598
cannot do the heavy parts of her trade.

00:00:05.598 --> 00:00:09.797
A partial disability benefit pays a flat percentage of the total disability

00:00:09.797 --> 00:00:13.646
benefit, commonly 50 percent, for a limited time, often six months.

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:17.845
Traditional partial disability benefits require that the insured first was totally disabled

00:00:17.845 --> 00:00:22.043
and then returned to work part time or in a reduced capacity.

00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:25.542
If Rosa’s total benefit is 3,600 dollars, her partial benefit

00:00:25.542 --> 00:00:29.041
is 1,800 dollars a month for up to six months.

00:00:29.041 --> 00:00:33.590
The flat percentage is simple but ignores how much income she actually lost.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:36.920
A residual disability benefit replaces the lost share

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.834
of income instead of a flat percentage.

00:00:39.834 --> 00:00:45.661
The formula is the percentage of income lost, multiplied by the total disability benefit.

00:00:45.661 --> 00:00:51.489
Rosa earned 6,000 dollars; working half days she earns 3,600, a 40 percent loss.

00:00:51.489 --> 00:00:56.900
Her residual benefit is 40 percent of 3,600, which is 1,440 dollars a

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:02.311
month, for as long as the income loss continues within the benefit period.

00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:07.306
Most residual provisions require at least a 20 percent income loss to

00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:12.301
pay and treat a loss of 75 or 80 percent as total.

00:01:12.301 --> 00:01:16.880
Residual benefits do not require a prior period of total disability.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.987
The two provisions answer the same situation differently.

00:01:19.987 --> 00:01:24.648
Partial pays a fixed fraction for a short time after total disability.

00:01:24.648 --> 00:01:29.308
Residual pays a proportion that tracks actual income loss for as long

00:01:29.308 --> 00:01:33.969
as the loss lasts, and it can begin without any total disability.

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:38.241
Residual coverage costs more and suits people whose disabilities tend to

00:01:38.241 --> 00:01:42.514
be gradual or whose return to work is slow, like Rosa.

00:01:42.514 --> 00:01:47.563
Income replacement contracts from Lesson 28 are built entirely around the residual idea.

00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:53.000
On a calculation question, identify which provision the policy uses before touching the numbers.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.782
Social Security disability takes months to approve and has a five-month waiting period.

00:01:57.782 --> 00:02:02.197
An additional monthly benefit rider pays an extra amount, say 1,200 dollars,

00:02:02.197 --> 00:02:06.611
during the first six to twelve months of a disability, when the

00:02:06.611 --> 00:02:10.290
insured is waiting to learn whether Social Security will pay.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.601
It is a short bridge, not a permanent increase.

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:17.647
If Rosa’s base benefit is 3,600 dollars and her AMB rider

00:02:17.647 --> 00:02:21.694
is 1,200, she receives 4,800 a month for the first year,

00:02:21.694 --> 00:02:25.005
then 3,600 whether or not Social Security approves her.

00:02:25.005 --> 00:02:27.580
The rider’s premium reflects its short duration.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:31.708
A social insurance supplement rider also adds a benefit,

00:02:31.708 --> 00:02:35.836
but its payment depends on what Social Security does.

00:02:35.836 --> 00:02:41.339
If Social Security denies the claim, the full SIS benefit is paid.

00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:47.760
If Social Security pays, the SIS benefit is reduced or eliminated dollar for dollar.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:52.347
The insured is protected against the strict federal definition without

00:02:52.347 --> 00:02:56.475
paying for a permanent benefit that duplicates Social Security.

00:02:56.475 --> 00:03:01.520
Group long-term disability plans use the same idea in reverse: they

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.565
pay a percentage of salary minus Social Security and workers compensation

00:03:06.565 --> 00:03:11.610
benefits, so the employee’s total income stays near the target percentage.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.339
Picture Rosa’s claim over two years.

00:03:14.339 --> 00:03:17.977
Months one through three: elimination period, no benefit.

00:03:17.977 --> 00:03:21.615
Months four through twelve: base benefit of 3,600

00:03:21.615 --> 00:03:24.799
plus an additional monthly benefit of 1,200.

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:29.802
Month twelve: Social Security approves 1,800 a month, so her SIS

00:03:29.802 --> 00:03:34.350
rider pays nothing, while the AMB rider ends on schedule.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:40.262
Month eighteen: she returns half time with a 40 percent income loss, and

00:03:40.262 --> 00:03:46.174
her residual benefit pays 1,440 until the loss ends or she reaches 65.

00:03:46.174 --> 00:03:52.086
Each provision solves one problem: the wait, the federal gap, the partial return.

00:03:52.086 --> 00:03:55.270
A good policy design anticipates all three.
